    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Since these are rare manuscripts, we're really worried about the \"chaos\" factor if we choose a system that's too broad. If we don't have a high level of granularity, we're afraid things will just get lost in the gaps or become a mess of uncatalogued items. Can you explain more about how these systems handle \"disorganized\" or loosely categorized materials compared to each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The way these systems handle \"disorganized\" material is quite different; both have designated areas for the unclassified, but they approach the problem with varying levels of structural rigidity. LCC, with its expansive alphanumeric structure, provides more specific \"catch-all\" areas, allowing you to place an item with a *very* specific, albeit unusual, subject heading. DDC, being more numerical, relies on broader ranges, which can feel more like a general \"pile\" if the material doesn't fit neatly into a pre-defined number.\n\nFor your rare manuscripts, if you have items that are truly anomalous, LCC's system allows you to build a more descriptive path to them, whereas DDC might force you into a broader, less informative bucket. It's less about the system *fixing* the chaos, and more about how well the system *accommodates* the chaos without letting it become a complete, unmanageable swamp of uncategorized relics. I'd advise leaning toward LCC's flexibility here.",
      "source": "target",
