    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That \"unmanageable swamp\" description is a bit concerning for our budget. If we go with LCC, how do we prevent that sense of things just being lost or becoming a chaotic mess in those catch-all areas? Is there a specific way librarians manage the \"anomalous\" items so they don't just end up as some sort of disorganized cluster?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The key to preventing the \"unmanageable swamp\" in LCC, especially with rare manuscripts, is rigorous application of controlled vocabulary and detailed descriptive metadata, which goes beyond just the classification number itself. Librarians manage anomalous items not by letting them drift, but by assigning them a *highly specific* sub-classification or using specific shelving protocols that flag them as exceptions. For instance, if a manuscript doesn't fit neatly into a known category, you don't just dump it in the general \"other\" area; you might assign it a unique local call number prefix that signals to staff, \"This item requires specialist review.\"\n\nThink of it this way: the classification number is the general location, but the detailed metadata—the subject headings, the physical description, the provenance—is the map that tells you *why* it's in that specific, slightly odd corner. This disciplined approach ensures that even the most peculiar item is documented, rather than just being a forgotten pile. This meticulous cataloging process is what keeps the collection organized, even when the contents are wild.",
      "source": "target",
